1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,989
It's Friday, November 13th, 2026

2
00:00:03,989 --> 00:00:06,213
at 3:54 p.m. Eastern.

3
00:00:06,563 --> 00:00:07,866
The FAA orders every

4
00:00:07,866 --> 00:00:09,365
airport in the country:

5
00:00:09,365 --> 00:00:12,037
hold all departures until further notice.

6
00:00:12,387 --> 00:00:14,230
The order cited "extraordinary

7
00:00:14,230 --> 00:00:16,627
circumstances" and "reasons of safety."

8
00:00:16,977 --> 00:00:18,247
It lasted four hours

9
00:00:18,247 --> 00:00:19,708
and twenty-one minutes.

10
00:00:20,058 --> 00:00:22,377
Over 3,500 planes were in the air.

11
00:00:22,377 --> 00:00:24,151
All of them landed safely.

12
00:00:24,501 --> 00:00:25,380
By that night

13
00:00:25,380 --> 00:00:27,545
over 4,400 flights were canceled

14
00:00:27,545 --> 00:00:29,169
and over 10,000 delayed.

15
00:00:29,519 --> 00:00:31,628
It was only the second nationwide

16
00:00:31,628 --> 00:00:33,098
ground stop since 9/11.

17
00:00:33,448 --> 00:00:35,707
So why? There are two answers out there

18
00:00:35,707 --> 00:00:37,444
and they sound like opposites.

19
00:00:37,794 --> 00:00:39,241
The system's too old.

20
00:00:39,241 --> 00:00:41,170
Or an update knocked it out.

21
00:00:41,520 --> 00:00:44,398
Our take? Both fall into the same trap.

22
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:53,804
Not the planes.

23
00:00:53,804 --> 00:00:54,834
Not the radar.

24
00:00:54,834 --> 00:00:56,158
A telecom network.

25
00:00:56,507 --> 00:00:57,833
It links over 4,400

26
00:00:57,833 --> 00:00:59,368
air traffic facilities

27
00:00:59,368 --> 00:01:01,670
including all 22 en route centers

28
00:01:01,670 --> 00:01:04,182
which guide planes between airports.

29
00:01:04,532 --> 00:01:06,632
Controllers' voices and radar

30
00:01:06,632 --> 00:01:08,080
data both run on it.

31
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:09,822
It's called FTI

32
00:01:09,822 --> 00:01:12,883
the FAA's communications backbone

33
00:01:12,883 --> 00:01:16,223
and a contractor, L3Harris, runs it.

34
00:01:16,573 --> 00:01:18,369
The FAA's statement was short:

35
00:01:18,369 --> 00:01:19,866
multiple en route centers

36
00:01:19,866 --> 00:01:21,303
had communication issues

37
00:01:21,303 --> 00:01:23,399
and technicians were investigating.

38
00:01:23,749 --> 00:01:26,932
Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy was blunter.

39
00:01:26,932 --> 00:01:29,584
He called it an "incredibly old" system.

40
00:01:29,934 --> 00:01:32,225
Old switches and routers, he said

41
00:01:32,225 --> 00:01:33,960
couldn't handle the data.

42
00:01:34,310 --> 00:01:35,607
And he stressed that none

43
00:01:35,607 --> 00:01:37,215
of the new equipment went down.

44
00:01:37,565 --> 00:01:40,554
Then he asked Congress for $50 billion.

45
00:01:40,904 --> 00:01:43,146
But CBS, citing unnamed sources

46
00:01:43,146 --> 00:01:44,882
said the network crashed

47
00:01:44,882 --> 00:01:46,980
after an update and a reboot.

48
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:48,729
That hasn't been confirmed.

49
00:01:49,079 --> 00:01:51,459
Homeland Security Secretary Markwayne

50
00:01:51,459 --> 00:01:53,260
Mullin split the difference.

51
00:01:53,260 --> 00:01:54,675
He said new technology

52
00:01:54,675 --> 00:01:56,733
was overwhelming the old system.

53
00:01:57,083 --> 00:01:58,350
As of November 14th

54
00:01:58,350 --> 00:02:00,084
there's no official cause.

55
00:02:00,434 --> 00:02:02,305
So we won't guess at this one.

56
00:02:02,305 --> 00:02:04,239
Let's look at the track record.

57
00:02:08,367 --> 00:02:09,905
Here's what we noticed about

58
00:02:09,905 --> 00:02:11,497
the worst nationwide failures

59
00:02:11,497 --> 00:02:12,925
in US air traffic control.

60
00:02:13,275 --> 00:02:15,378
Each time, someone was in the middle

61
00:02:15,378 --> 00:02:16,663
of changing something.

62
00:02:17,013 --> 00:02:18,118
In November 2009

63
00:02:18,118 --> 00:02:19,707
it was the same network

64
00:02:19,707 --> 00:02:21,089
and the same company

65
00:02:21,089 --> 00:02:23,230
back when it was called Harris.

66
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:25,542
They were moving data off the old

67
00:02:25,542 --> 00:02:27,267
network onto new fiber lines.

68
00:02:27,617 --> 00:02:28,416
In Los Angeles

69
00:02:28,416 --> 00:02:29,956
a temporary router bridging

70
00:02:29,956 --> 00:02:31,895
the two networks was set up wrong.

71
00:02:32,245 --> 00:02:33,598
Then the mistake was copied

72
00:02:33,598 --> 00:02:34,600
onto the new router.

73
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:38,022
The damage should have stayed contained.

74
00:02:38,022 --> 00:02:40,402
Instead, it hit 129 facilities.

75
00:02:40,752 --> 00:02:42,952
The outage lasted five hours

76
00:02:42,952 --> 00:02:44,838
and delayed 820 flights.

77
00:02:45,188 --> 00:02:46,598
That's from the Transportation

78
00:02:46,598 --> 00:02:48,056
Department's inspector general.

79
00:02:48,406 --> 00:02:51,639
August 2015. Washington's air traffic center

80
00:02:51,639 --> 00:02:53,916
had just upgraded its software.

81
00:02:54,266 --> 00:02:56,453
A new feature filled up the memory.

82
00:02:56,453 --> 00:02:58,390
Almost 500 flights were delayed

83
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:00,140
and nearly as many canceled.

84
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:03,005
January 2023. The system that sends

85
00:03:03,005 --> 00:03:04,801
notices to pilots crashed

86
00:03:04,801 --> 00:03:07,316
and the whole country was grounded.

87
00:03:07,666 --> 00:03:09,264
A contractor had deleted

88
00:03:09,264 --> 00:03:10,929
files during maintenance.

89
00:03:11,279 --> 00:03:13,519
Congress's watchdog, the GAO

90
00:03:13,519 --> 00:03:16,639
says that system was over 30 years old.

91
00:03:16,989 --> 00:03:19,144
Too old, or someone touched it?

92
00:03:19,144 --> 00:03:20,813
Picking one is the trap.

93
00:03:20,813 --> 00:03:22,064
Here, it was both.

94
00:03:22,414 --> 00:03:25,364
This time might be the same. Might.

95
00:03:29,467 --> 00:03:31,323
Air traffic control never stops.

96
00:03:31,323 --> 00:03:33,586
You can't just reboot it like a laptop.

97
00:03:33,936 --> 00:03:35,767
You have to upgrade it mid-flight

98
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:38,041
a bit like swapping an engine in the air.

99
00:03:38,391 --> 00:03:40,152
The new parts come in bit by bit

100
00:03:40,152 --> 00:03:41,912
while the old ones keep running.

101
00:03:42,262 --> 00:03:43,374
The way we see it

102
00:03:43,374 --> 00:03:45,924
that overlap is when it's most fragile.

103
00:03:46,274 --> 00:03:47,535
And the older it gets

104
00:03:47,535 --> 00:03:49,337
the fewer people understand it

105
00:03:49,337 --> 00:03:51,079
so every change gets riskier.

106
00:03:51,429 --> 00:03:53,645
Riskier means nobody dares touch it.

107
00:03:53,645 --> 00:03:55,553
So it just keeps getting older.

108
00:03:55,903 --> 00:03:57,820
We'd call that technical debt.

109
00:03:57,820 --> 00:03:58,459
Put it off

110
00:03:58,459 --> 00:04:00,567
and the interest keeps piling up.

111
00:04:00,917 --> 00:04:03,115
The evidence? In 2024

112
00:04:03,115 --> 00:04:06,465
the GAO checked 138 of the FAA's

113
00:04:06,465 --> 00:04:08,559
air traffic systems.

114
00:04:08,909 --> 00:04:11,097
51 couldn't be kept going.

115
00:04:11,097 --> 00:04:13,117
Another 54 might not be.

116
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:16,328
This September, the FAA's own administrator

117
00:04:16,328 --> 00:04:18,391
Bryan Bedford, put it this way:

118
00:04:19,241 --> 00:04:20,601
"We're pulling copper up

119
00:04:20,601 --> 00:04:21,848
and putting fiber down

120
00:04:21,848 --> 00:04:23,831
but it's the same data architecture

121
00:04:23,831 --> 00:04:25,475
that we've had for 50 years."

122
00:04:25,825 --> 00:04:27,626
And the overlap keeps stretching.

123
00:04:27,976 --> 00:04:30,118
The contract for the next

124
00:04:30,118 --> 00:04:32,860
network went to Verizon in 2023.

125
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:35,944
This August, AT&T took over as lead.

126
00:04:36,294 --> 00:04:39,918
Meanwhile, the old FTI network has to keep running.

127
00:04:44,033 --> 00:04:44,726
To be fair

128
00:04:44,726 --> 00:04:47,360
it's not always a change that does it.

129
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:49,496
In Dallas in September 2025

130
00:04:49,496 --> 00:04:51,149
two fiber lines were cut.

131
00:04:51,149 --> 00:04:53,398
The FAA says the contractor hadn't

132
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:55,514
made sure the backup would work.

133
00:04:55,864 --> 00:04:57,788
In Philadelphia this September

134
00:04:57,788 --> 00:04:59,905
the main line failed. The backup?

135
00:04:59,905 --> 00:05:01,317
Construction crews had

136
00:05:01,317 --> 00:05:02,792
already cut through it.

137
00:05:03,142 --> 00:05:04,027
Back in 2004

138
00:05:04,027 --> 00:05:06,610
Los Angeles had a different outage.

139
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:08,306
The FAA says a required

140
00:05:08,306 --> 00:05:09,782
restart was skipped.

141
00:05:10,132 --> 00:05:11,430
The system crashed.

142
00:05:11,430 --> 00:05:13,820
The backup crashed within a minute.

143
00:05:14,170 --> 00:05:16,084
And FTI adds 150 to 200

144
00:05:16,084 --> 00:05:18,081
new connections a month.

145
00:05:18,081 --> 00:05:20,328
Almost all of them go fine.

146
00:05:20,678 --> 00:05:21,903
So we're only talking

147
00:05:21,903 --> 00:05:23,362
about the worst failures.

148
00:05:23,712 --> 00:05:25,507
Look at how those backups failed.

149
00:05:25,857 --> 00:05:27,287
A backup protects you when

150
00:05:27,287 --> 00:05:28,551
things fail separately.

151
00:05:28,551 --> 00:05:30,091
Not when they fail together.

152
00:05:30,441 --> 00:05:33,845
Same trench, same network, same contractor?

153
00:05:33,845 --> 00:05:36,774
Then the backup only exists on paper.

154
00:05:37,124 --> 00:05:38,578
As for why one fault went

155
00:05:38,578 --> 00:05:39,741
nationwide this time

156
00:05:39,741 --> 00:05:42,125
we'll have to wait for the investigation.

157
00:05:46,233 --> 00:05:47,967
Critics would say we're just

158
00:05:47,967 --> 00:05:49,454
making excuses to stall.

159
00:05:49,804 --> 00:05:51,806
Duffy himself said the new gear

160
00:05:51,806 --> 00:05:53,614
held and the old gear broke.

161
00:05:53,964 --> 00:05:57,351
They'd say: spend more and get it replaced faster.

162
00:05:57,701 --> 00:05:59,551
And saying upgrades are risky

163
00:05:59,551 --> 00:06:01,018
just scares people off.

164
00:06:01,968 --> 00:06:04,752
We agree it has to be replaced. And fast.

165
00:06:05,102 --> 00:06:06,718
But we think the switchover

166
00:06:06,718 --> 00:06:08,334
itself is the riskiest part

167
00:06:08,334 --> 00:06:09,352
so plan for that.

168
00:06:09,702 --> 00:06:11,294
The longer it drags on

169
00:06:11,294 --> 00:06:13,898
the longer that risky overlap lasts.

170
00:06:14,248 --> 00:06:16,424
And money alone doesn't buy safety.

171
00:06:16,774 --> 00:06:19,967
This September, the GAO found no reliable estimate

172
00:06:19,967 --> 00:06:22,138
of the new system's lifetime cost.

173
00:06:22,488 --> 00:06:24,483
And the project runs on over 11,000

174
00:06:24,483 --> 00:06:26,933
separate schedules that don't fit together.

175
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:32,143
One last angle.

176
00:06:32,143 --> 00:06:34,364
A nationwide ground stop looks

177
00:06:34,364 --> 00:06:36,585
like the system falling apart.

178
00:06:36,935 --> 00:06:38,526
We'd argue it's the safety

179
00:06:38,526 --> 00:06:39,750
brake doing its job.

180
00:06:40,100 --> 00:06:42,200
When the system can't be sure

181
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,010
it holds every departure.

182
00:06:44,010 --> 00:06:45,241
Costly, but safe.

183
00:06:45,591 --> 00:06:46,794
And during the stop

184
00:06:46,794 --> 00:06:49,138
every plane in the air landed safely.

185
00:06:49,488 --> 00:06:51,564
We think braking feels like failure

186
00:06:51,564 --> 00:06:53,581
because you can see what it costs.

187
00:06:53,931 --> 00:06:55,761
You can't see what it prevented.

188
00:06:56,111 --> 00:06:58,379
If you can't live without a system

189
00:06:58,379 --> 00:06:59,779
getting old is risky.

190
00:06:59,779 --> 00:07:01,313
Touching it is riskier.

191
00:07:01,663 --> 00:07:03,384
So don't leave it alone.

192
00:07:03,384 --> 00:07:06,253
Just be extra careful when you touch it.
